Create .env File
In the root folder of your project, create a file named:
.env

Add the following configuration:
# Server
PORT=5000
NODE_ENV=development

# MongoDB
MONGODB_URI=mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017/product_inventory

# JWT Authentication
JWT_SECRET=your_super_secret_jwt_key_change_this
JWT_EXPIRES_IN=7d

Project Structure
product-inventory-management/
│
├── src/
│   ├── app.js
│   ├── config/
│   ├── controllers/
│   ├── models/
│   ├── routes/
│   └── middleware/
│
├── .env
├── .gitignore
├── package.json
└── README.md

Add .env to .gitignore
Create or update .gitignore:

node_modules/
.env

Important: Never commit .env to GitHub because it can contain database credentials and authentication secrets.

Load Environment Variables
At the beginning of src/app.js:

require("dotenv").config();

const express = require("express");

const app = express();

app.use(express.json());

const PORT = process.env.PORT || 5000;

app.listen(PORT, () => {
    console.log(`Server running on port ${PORT}`);
});

You can then access the variables anywhere in your backend with:

process.env.PORT
process.env.MONGODB_URI
process.env.JWT_SECRET

For example:

const mongoose = require("mongoose");

mongoose.connect(process.env.MONGODB_URI)
    .then(() => console.log("MongoDB connected"))
    .catch((error) => console.error("MongoDB connection failed:", err
